<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Sending Apple events from this process.

    The app is signed with the hardened runtime, which blocks Apple events outright
    unless this is present — and the failure is the worst shape available: macOS returns
    errAEEventNotPermitted *without ever showing the consent dialog*. The permission
    therefore cannot be granted, from inside the app or from System Settings, because
    OpenBoard never appears under Automation for having never successfully asked.

    That is not theoretical. Setup's Grant button returned denied instantly, ten times
    in five seconds, on a machine where the record had just been cleared with
    `tccutil reset AppleEvents`. Nothing was refused; nothing was ever asked.

    Note this is separate from the automation the app does day to day, which runs
    through /usr/bin/osascript as a subprocess. osascript carries this entitlement
    itself, which is why jumping to a chat has always worked and why the gap stayed
    invisible for so long — every path that used Apple events borrowed someone else's
    permission to send them.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
